fh439@ncf.ca (Shawn I Roske) writes:
> gotten so far is 10th DoD and about as far in the mines, I've been up
> a few flights of Sokoban, and I found some other tower that looked
> like Sokoban, but I didn't die there and had to turn back when I was
> blind and accidentally pushed 2 boulders in my way.

AFAIK, there's no way to make Sokoban truly unsolvable. (You can
certainly make it not worth the effort and/or cause yourself all
manner of grief in the attempt.)

Also AFAIK, there is nothing in the vicinity of Dlvl 10 which looks
like Sokoban without actually being Sokoban.

> Anyway, RNG has been giving me wishes lately and I'm not sure what to
> wish for.

If you're playing unspoiled, one good approach is to wish for the most
helpful thing you've seen in any of your games thus far. A somewhat
more spohisticated approach is to do likewise, but also taking into
account your biggest weakness or worst problem in the current game.

> I saw the name stormbringer once, wished for it from a fountain, and
> got it.  It was fun to swing that sword around, but my ranger didn't
> seem to really get much out of it.

Stormy is a powerful weapon, but has drawbacks. There are more
powerful weapons, and other things besides weapons that are likely
better early-game wishes in most situations.

> I'm wondering what are good things to wish for.  If I wished for a
> wand of wishes I wonder if I would get it?  I tried dragonscale armor,
> but the wand gave me a cape of magic resistance.

The wish-parser isn't that smart. You need to pay careful attention to
_exactly_ how things are described in your inventory, and then wish
for that exact thing letter-for-letter (at least until you are way
more spoiled than you are now).

> I asked for an amulet of telepathy and got an amulet of detect
> invisible, and I wished for a ring of identify and got a ring of
> not-needing-air or something.

Wishes are powerful, but cannot create things which don't exist in
the world in which nethack is set. 

> Are wishes generally random? 

Wishes are random if you give them the chance to be. If I wish for a
"ring of fondue fork mastery" then I'll get a ring, but it won't be
the one I asked for.

> I got stormbringer from a fountain.  Perhaps wands are not as
> powerful as fountains.

Neat idea, but at present all wishes are created equal (at least 
in regard what stuff you get when you use them).

> I haven't tried explore mode.  Is it worth it to use that mode?

Starting a game in explore mode gives you a wand of wishing with three
charges. It's a much faster way to experiment with wishes than hoping
for them to pop up when you play "for real".

Severe spoilers ahead:

http://nh.gmuf.com/wish.html has oceans of detail.

If I were to get an early-game wish and had no especially exciting
equipment or problems yet, I'd probably wish for:

   blessed fixed +3 gray dragon scale mail

There are certainly other useful choices, and context is everything;
there is no one best thing for which to wish. (Indeed, you'll doubtless
see a dozen posts downthread explaining why GDSM is a waste and you
should get some other thing instead.)

Also: Remember that saving wishes for future use may be smart, but
dying with unused wishes in hand is always dumb.


